Let $\varphi : R 	o S$ be a ring map. Let $\mathfrak p$ be a prime of $R$. The following are equivalent $\mathfrak p$ is in the image of $\Spec(S) 	o \Spec(R)$, $S \otimes_R \kappa(\mathfrak p) 
ot = 0$, $S_{\mathfrak p}/\mathfrak p S_{\mathfrak p} 
ot = 0$, $(S/\mathfrak pS)_{\mathfrak p} 
ot = 0$, and $\mathfrak p = \varphi^{-1}(\mathfrak pS)$.

Let $A 	o B$ be a ring map. Let $\Omega_{B/A} 	o \Omega$ be a quotient satisfying the assumptions of Algebra, Lemma ef{algebra-lemma-de-rham-complex}. Let $M$ be a $B$-module. A {\it connection} is an additive map $$ 
abla : M \longrightarrow M \otimes_B \Omega $$ such that $
abla(bm) = b 
abla(m) + m \otimes 	ext{d}b$ for $b \in B$ and $m \in M$. In this situation we can define maps $$ 
abla : M \otimes_B \Omega^i \longrightarrow M \otimes_B \Omega^{i + 1} $$ by the rule $
abla(m \otimes \omega) = 
abla(m) \wedge \omega + m \otimes 	ext{d}\omega$. This works because if $b \in B$, then  egin{align*} 
abla(bm \otimes \omega) - 
abla(m \otimes b\omega) & = 
abla(bm) \wedge \omega + bm \otimes 	ext{d}\omega - 
abla(m) \wedge b\omega - m \otimes 	ext{d}(b\omega) \ & = b
abla(m) \wedge \omega + m \otimes 	ext{d}b \wedge \omega + bm \otimes 	ext{d}\omega \ & \ \ \ \ \ \ - b
abla(m) \wedge \omega - bm \otimes 	ext{d}(\omega) - m \otimes 	ext{d}b \wedge \omega = 0 \end{align*} As is customary we say the connection is {\it integrable} if and only if the composition $$ M \xrightarrow{
abla} M \otimes_B \Omega^1 \xrightarrow{
abla} M \otimes_B \Omega^2 $$ is zero. In this case we obtain a complex $$ M \xrightarrow{
abla} M \otimes_B \Omega^1 \xrightarrow{
abla} M \otimes_B \Omega^2 \xrightarrow{
abla} M \otimes_B \Omega^3 \xrightarrow{
abla} M \otimes_B \Omega^4 	o \ldots $$ which is called the de Rham complex of the connection.

Let $$ \xymatrix{ Y_2 \ar[d]_{f_2} \ar[r] & Y_1 \ar[d]^{f_1} \ X_2 \ar[r] & X_1 } $$ be a commutative diagram of morphisms of schemes. Assume $f_1$, $f_2$ quasi-compact and quasi-separated. Let $f_i = 
u_i \circ f_i'$, $i = 1, 2$ be the canonical factorizations, where $
u_i : X_i' 	o X_i$ is the normalization of $X_i$ in $Y_i$. Then there exists a unique arrow $X'_2 	o X'_1$ fitting into a commutative diagram $$ \xymatrix{ Y_2 \ar[d]_{f_2'} \ar[r] & Y_1 \ar[d]^{f_1'} \ X_2' \ar[d]_{
u_2} \ar[r] & X_1' \ar[d]^{
u_1} \ X_2 \ar[r] & X_1 } $$
